WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:04.234
-I'm so happy you're here. Here, please, have a seat.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:07.000
I'm so happy. Happy you're here. Thanks so much for --

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.467
Last time I saw you, I was --

00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:11.234
I went backstage to say what a great job you did.

00:00:11.234 --> 00:00:13.334
-You did. -"To Kill a Mockingbird."

00:00:13.334 --> 00:00:15.634
You crushed it. There you are. There we are.

00:00:15.634 --> 00:00:16.701
There's a photo of us.

00:00:16.701 --> 00:00:18.100
-Remember you know who that is, sir?

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.267
-Wait. Don't tell me. Wait.

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:20.534
This is very -- very interesting.

00:00:20.534 --> 00:00:22.968
Yes, I do remember who that was. -Come on, come on.

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:24.868
-It was... -Old-school.

00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:26.200
-...Gregory Peck's... -That's it.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:28.267
-...grandson? -And look at him.

00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:30.267
-He looks like -- -If there's anyone

00:00:30.267 --> 00:00:32.167
who's over 50,

00:00:32.167 --> 00:00:35.501
you might remember what Gregory Peck looks like.

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.534
He looks just like him. -Yeah.

00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.400
And he was at "To Kill a Mockingbird."

00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.400
Did you know he was there when you were --

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:43.901
-No, we didn't. He came backstage.

00:00:43.901 --> 00:00:45.267
Glenn Close.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:47.267
-Oh, Glenn Close. That's right. -This is Glenn Close.

00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:49.033
-Glenn Close was there. I mean -- I feel like --

00:00:49.033 --> 00:00:50.300
When you're doing the show,

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:52.434
do you find out who is in the audience ever?

00:00:52.434 --> 00:00:54.501
Do you like knowing that or you don't like?

00:00:54.501 --> 00:00:56.367
-You know, I don't really care. -Yeah!

00:00:56.367 --> 00:00:58.200
That's what I'm talking about!

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:00.200
You just go out and you go, "What's up?"

00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:02.501
You just crush it anyway. -No.

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:05.267
You're supposed to be so invested in the moment,

00:01:05.267 --> 00:01:07.367
in the truth of what you're doing,

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:09.267
which is why I'm a director...

00:01:09.267 --> 00:01:11.100
-Yeah! -...that you don't --

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:15.400
You know, actors who get involved in who's out there,

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.934
that's a different energy that you don't need

00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:20.234
because you need your mind in the moment

00:01:20.234 --> 00:01:21.634
of what you're doing because

00:01:21.634 --> 00:01:25.367
that's the quickest way to go up, to forget a line,

00:01:25.367 --> 00:01:28.267
is to start thinking outside of where you are.

00:01:28.267 --> 00:01:29.968
-No. Just be in it. Oh, I love this.

00:01:29.968 --> 00:01:32.200
I love getting direction from you. This is awesome.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.234
-I'm going to direct you in something, Jimmy.

00:01:34.234 --> 00:01:35.601
-I love it. I would love --

00:01:35.601 --> 00:01:38.801
-But it won't be crushing eggs on our head.

00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:40.033
[ Laughter ]

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:43.367
-Hey. I got to make a living. Come on.

00:01:43.367 --> 00:01:46.567
You know, we had your husband on the show a few months ago,

00:01:46.567 --> 00:01:49.133
Samuel L. Jackson, and whose -- We love him.

00:01:49.133 --> 00:01:50.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:50.534 --> 00:01:52.367
He's a great guest. Always been great to us.

00:01:52.367 --> 00:01:55.767
And he said -- he said to -- to us all

00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:57.434
that you were throwing away --

00:01:57.434 --> 00:01:59.968
You threw away, like, 500 of his Kangol hats.

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:02.267
-Well, why did he think that was important?

00:02:02.267 --> 00:02:05.033
Everybody's purging. Do you know how old we are?

00:02:05.033 --> 00:02:09.100
What the heck are you gonna do with 500 Kangol hats?

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.934
-Dude, you can't -- -He can't wear them.

00:02:10.934 --> 00:02:12.968
He doesn't wear them. He wears them once.

00:02:12.968 --> 00:02:15.734
I mean, you give away, and then I end up, like,

00:02:15.734 --> 00:02:19.400
packing everything up to, like, take it to a place to see...

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:22.133
A thousand sneakers?

00:02:22.133 --> 00:02:23.501
-We didn't get -- -1,000 pair of sneakers.

00:02:23.501 --> 00:02:24.934
-We didn't even get into the sneakers.

00:02:24.934 --> 00:02:26.000
-We not getting into it now.

00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.000
[ Laughter ]

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.567
I didn't even say it. It just came out of my mouth.

00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:32.901
-It just -- "I didn't even say it." Yeah.

00:02:32.901 --> 00:02:34.868
What was your first impression of Sam when you --

00:02:34.868 --> 00:02:36.400
when you met each other?

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:38.767
-Oh, man. Are you kidding? He was so fine.

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:40.200
Do you remember --

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:43.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:43.334 --> 00:02:44.868
I have to -- I want to --

00:02:44.868 --> 00:02:48.567
You know, I talk old-school 'cause I'm 73.

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:51.601
So I think about people who are back --

00:02:51.601 --> 00:02:54.501
You know, from back in the day, you think about Linc Hayes.

00:02:54.501 --> 00:02:57.868
Clarence Williams III has passed on now, but...

00:02:57.868 --> 00:03:01.367
Linc Hayes. Sam looked exactly like Linc Hayes.

00:03:01.367 --> 00:03:03.334
He had a huge afro

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:07.033
and these little glasses and he was really tall.

00:03:07.033 --> 00:03:09.834
I think he's lost an inch or so, but about --

00:03:09.834 --> 00:03:12.400
-"The Mod Squad." -6'4". "Mod Squad."

00:03:12.400 --> 00:03:13.968
Looking -- You know what I mean? -Yeah.

00:03:13.968 --> 00:03:17.200
-Just down. He was, you know, a brother.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.167
-Yeah, and you were like --

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.067
You're like, "Oh, that's -- I think I see something there."

00:03:22.067 --> 00:03:25.234
-Well, not quite like that. But --

00:03:25.234 --> 00:03:27.367
I said, "I could work with that."

00:03:27.367 --> 00:03:29.567
[ Laughter ]

00:03:29.567 --> 00:03:31.634
-Yeah.

00:03:31.634 --> 00:03:33.267
-It was something.

00:03:33.267 --> 00:03:34.934
-And now -- How long has it been?

00:03:34.934 --> 00:03:37.501
Been 40? -50...yeah.

00:03:37.501 --> 00:03:40.434
-Really? -54 years.

00:03:40.434 --> 00:03:42.534
-Wow. Wow.

00:03:42.534 --> 00:03:44.234
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:44.234 --> 00:03:46.267
So -- So you like him.

00:03:46.267 --> 00:03:50.567
-That's an -- That's an entire show.

00:03:50.567 --> 00:03:52.634
-So you like him. You like him. You like him.

00:03:52.634 --> 00:03:54.234
-I like him. -Yeah.

00:03:54.234 --> 00:03:57.167
You directed this play right here, "The Piano Lesson."

00:03:57.167 --> 00:03:59.234
Congratulations. You're getting rave reviews.

00:03:59.234 --> 00:04:00.434
-Thank you.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:01.467
-Can you explain what the show is about?

00:04:01.467 --> 00:04:02.968
-You know, it's -- it's about two --

00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:07.868
a brother and a sister who are locked in this battle of legacy

00:04:07.868 --> 00:04:10.267
versus a stake in the American dream.

00:04:10.267 --> 00:04:15.367
And the whole idea, I hope, that everyone will leave with

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.701
is trying to decide either they're Team Bernice,

00:04:18.701 --> 00:04:21.067
who wants to keep the legacy and keep the piano

00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.033
and keep the history of the family...

00:04:23.033 --> 00:04:27.634
or Team Boy Willie, who says, "That's going to get us nowhere.

00:04:27.634 --> 00:04:31.067
I can go and buy the land, farm the land,

00:04:31.067 --> 00:04:34.467
and make something out of that for us and our family

00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:36.133
for the next proceeding years."

00:04:36.133 --> 00:04:38.067
So that's what it's about.

00:04:38.067 --> 00:04:40.467
It's the -- it's the battle for the piano.

00:04:40.467 --> 00:04:43.534
-And you're the first female director

00:04:43.534 --> 00:04:45.267
to direct an August Wilson play.

00:04:45.267 --> 00:04:47.767
-For shame. For shame. Yeah.

00:04:47.767 --> 00:04:49.667
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:51.868
For shame.

00:04:51.868 --> 00:04:55.701
We're still -- You know, we're still in a struggle

00:04:55.701 --> 00:04:58.434
in this country with women to --

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:01.000
for us to own ourselves.

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:03.300
And that's loaded. So, yes.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.067
For us to own our bodies and to own ourselves.

00:05:06.067 --> 00:05:08.634
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:08.634 --> 00:05:14.601
So this -- It's sad that men have been unwilling to share.

00:05:14.601 --> 00:05:16.100
But you know what's getting ready to happen.

00:05:16.100 --> 00:05:18.000
We're going to turn this thing upside down and you're going to

00:05:18.000 --> 00:05:20.334
have to share or move over.

00:05:20.334 --> 00:05:21.968
-Yeah. [ Laughs ]

00:05:21.968 --> 00:05:25.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.734
I'm so happy you came on the show tonight,

00:05:27.734 --> 00:05:29.267
and you crushed it.

00:05:29.267 --> 00:05:31.167
Please come back. And congratulations on the play.

00:05:31.167 --> 00:05:33.601
-Thank you for having me. What a great audience.

00:05:33.601 --> 00:05:35.434
Thank you...

00:05:35.434 --> 00:05:38.167
-Are you kidding me? Latanya Richardson Jackson.

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:40.000
Check out "The Piano Lesson"

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:43.734
through January 15th at the Barrymore Theatre.

00:05:43.734 --> 00:05:46.000
We'll be right back with a performance

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.067
from Joey Bada$$ with Men I Trust.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:49.467
Stick around.

